.TH EUCLID-CLI-ESM-STOP-BUCKET 1 "October 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-esm-stop-bucket \- hold a bucket's events until it is started again
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli esm stop-bucket
.BR \-\-bucket | \-b
.I NAME|ERN
.SH DESCRIPTION
Stops a bucket's events. The objects are untouched: they are put, read,
moved and deleted exactly as before. Every event about them is still made
and stored in EES, for every subscriber it would have reached, but
.BR held :
no queue subscribed to the bucket is sent one, no
.B ees receive-events
returns one, and none of them expires, however long the bucket stays
stopped.
.PP
The deliveries to the bucket's subscribers that were still waiting when it
was stopped are held too. A delivery already being handed over finishes.
.PP
What it is for: a consumer downstream is broken, or being redeployed, and
what arrives meanwhile should wait for it rather than be lost or pile up as
failures.
.PP
.BR euclid-cli-esm-start-bucket (1)
lets them all go, oldest first.
.B esm get-bucket
shows
.B stopped
for a stopped bucket.
.SH CAVEATS
A subscriber that is only pushed events live, and stores none, gets none of
those made while the bucket is stopped: there is nothing to hold them in.
.PP
Events of the bucket that were already waiting for an EES subscriber, as
opposed to a subscribed queue, when the bucket was stopped are delivered: only
the ones made after the stop are held.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bucket " " \fINAME|ERN\fR
The bucket. Required.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The bucket is stopped. The answer says how many events it now holds.
.TP
.B 1
No such bucket, not permitted, or the server failed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-esm-start-bucket (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm (1)
